hey this is presh talwalkar reminding  you to mind your decisions  in the ncaa march madness tournament a  bracket typically consists of 64  basketball teams playing single  elimination games until there's a single  champion  the question is what are the odds of  getting all of the picks perfectly by  pure luck it's an interesting  mathematical exercise that i want to go  over  so one way to approach this problem is  to first count the number of games  we can do this by counting the number of  games in each round  the first round involves 32 games  because each of the 64 teams is paired  up with another team  now each subsequent round will involve  half the number of games because half of  the teams are eliminated in each round  so the second round involves 16 teams  the third round involves eight teams and  so on and we keep having the number of  games until we get to just one game  where we have a champion  we can get the total number of games by  adding up these numbers and we get 63  games  now i want to mention this is the long  way to do this  the shortcut is you can notice that all  64 teams lose exactly once except the  champion that's another way that you can  see there are 63 games  and you can generalize this logic that  in a tournament of two to the end teams  with no buys there must be two to the n  minus one games played in total it's a  nice little combinatorial identity that  we learn from march madness  so now let's calculate the odds if each  pick has a 50 chance or probability of  one half of being correct by pure luck  well picking one game will have a chance  of one half  so we need to pick 63 games which will  be one-half to the power of 63  this equals 1 over about 9.2 quintillion  which is an astronomically large number  because it's so hard to pick a perfect  bracket that's part of the appeal of  march madness  good luck with your picks  thanks for watching these math videos  available for free on youtube build  confidence for students and i